<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE topic
        SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       title="Use cases" id="Use-cases">

    <web-summary>
        Follow Acme and its three dev teams: team workspaces, shared libraries, a personal global workspace and a
        company workspace that onboards newcomers.
    </web-summary>

    <p>
        Acme has three development teams. This story follows how they use %tool_name% as the company grows.
    </p>

    <chapter title="One workspace per team" id="use-cases_chapter-teams">
        <table>
            <tr>
                <td>Team</td>
                <td>Workspace repository</td>
                <td>Projects</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>dev1</td>
                <td><code>ws-dev1</code></td>
                <td><code>shop-back</code>, <code>shop-front</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>dev2</td>
                <td><code>ws-dev2</code></td>
                <td><code>billing-back</code>, <code>billing-front</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>dev3</td>
                <td><code>ws-dev3</code></td>
                <td><code>lib-back</code>, <code>lib-front</code> (libraries shared by back and front)</td>
            </tr>
        </table>
        <p>
            A workspace is a git repository that only contains the list of its projects. Here is
            <path>ws-dev1/.gws/workspaces.yaml</path>:
        </p>
        <code-block lang="yaml"><![CDATA[
version: 1
self:
  remotes:
    - url: git@git.acme.dev:acme/ws-dev1.git
projects:
  - path: shop-back
    url: git@git.acme.dev:acme/shop-back.git
  - path: shop-front
    url: git@git.acme.dev:acme/shop-front.git
]]></code-block>
        <p>
            The team runs <code>%tool_call% init gitignore</code> once, so the workspace repository tracks only its
            configuration and ignores the cloned projects.
        </p>
        <p>A new developer joins dev1. Two commands give them the whole team:</p>
        <code-block lang="shell"><![CDATA[
git clone git@git.acme.dev:acme/ws-dev1.git
cd ws-dev1
gogws update
]]></code-block>
        <p>
            <code>%tool_call% status</code> shows the state of the projects. Every morning,
            <code>%tool_call% fetch</code> and <code>%tool_call% ff</code> update them all in parallel.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="dev1 needs the shared libraries" id="use-cases_chapter-libs">
        <p>
            The shop projects use the libraries of dev3. dev1 adds the workspace of dev3 as a sub-workspace in
            <path>ws-dev1/.gws/workspaces.yaml</path> and pushes:
        </p>
        <code-block lang="yaml"><![CDATA[
workspaces:
  - path: libs
    url: git@git.acme.dev:acme/ws-dev3.git
]]></code-block>
        <p>Each teammate pulls, then runs one command:</p>
        <code-block lang="shell"><![CDATA[
git pull
gogws update -r
]]></code-block>
        <p><code>-r</code> clones the new workspace, then the projects inside it.</p>
        <code-block lang="shell"><![CDATA[
ws-dev1/
├── .gws/
├── shop-back/
├── shop-front/
└── libs/
    ├── .gws/
    ├── lib-back/
    └── lib-front/
]]></code-block>
        <p>
            <code>%tool_call% status</code> now shows <code>libs</code> under <code>ws-dev1</code>, and
            <code>%tool_call% search lib</code> finds <code>lib-back</code> and <code>lib-front</code>.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="A personal shortcut" id="use-cases_chapter-global">
        <p>
            Some developers want the libraries at hand from any folder, for example to open them in an editor.
            dev1 must not force this on the whole team, so they use the global workspace. It is a file in their
            own home folder, outside any repository:
        </p>
        <p><path>~/.gws/global.workspaces.yaml</path></p>
        <code-block lang="yaml"><![CDATA[
version: 1
workspaces:
  - folder: ~/work/ws-dev1/libs
]]></code-block>
        <p>
            Paths in this file are absolute or start with <code>~/</code>. A <code>folder</code> is an existing
            directory that %tool_name% loads, with the projects it contains.
        </p>
        <p>From any folder that is not in a workspace:</p>
        <code-block lang="shell"><![CDATA[
$ gogws search lib
project    lib-back   ~/work/ws-dev1/libs/lib-back
project    lib-front  ~/work/ws-dev1/libs/lib-front
workspace  libs       ~/work/ws-dev1/libs

$ gcd lib-front
$ pwd
~/work/ws-dev1/libs/lib-front
]]></code-block>
        <p>
            <code>gcd</code> is described in <a href="Scope.topic">Scope</a>.
        </p>
        <table>
            <tr>
                <td></td>
                <td>Workspace file</td>
                <td>Global workspace</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Lives in</td>
                <td>The workspace repository</td>
                <td>Your home folder</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Shared with</td>
                <td>The whole team</td>
                <td>Only you</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Used for</td>
                <td>What the team works on</td>
                <td>What you want to reach from anywhere</td>
            </tr>
        </table>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Everybody works on the libraries" id="use-cases_chapter-company">
        <p>
            Soon dev2 needs the libraries too. Acme creates a company workspace: a repository
            <code>ws-acme</code> that lists the three team workspaces.
        </p>
        <code-block lang="yaml"><![CDATA[
version: 1
self:
  remotes:
    - url: git@git.acme.dev:acme/ws-acme.git
workspaces:
  - path: ws-dev1
    url: git@git.acme.dev:acme/ws-dev1.git
  - path: ws-dev2
    url: git@git.acme.dev:acme/ws-dev2.git
  - path: ws-dev3
    url: git@git.acme.dev:acme/ws-dev3.git
]]></code-block>
        <p>
            dev1 removes its <code>libs</code> entry. The libraries now exist once, in <code>ws-dev3</code>.
        </p>
        <code-block lang="mermaid"><![CDATA[
flowchart TD
    acme["ws-acme<br/>company workspace"] --> d1["ws-dev1"]
    acme --> d2["ws-dev2"]
    acme --> d3["ws-dev3"]
    d1 --> sb["shop-back"]
    d1 --> sf["shop-front"]
    d2 --> bb["billing-back"]
    d2 --> bf["billing-front"]
    d3 --> lb["lib-back"]
    d3 --> lf["lib-front"]
    g["global workspace<br/>personal"] -.-> acme
]]></code-block>
        <p>A newcomer, whatever their team, now needs two commands for the six projects of the company:</p>
        <code-block lang="shell"><![CDATA[
git clone git@git.acme.dev:acme/ws-acme.git
cd ws-acme
gogws update -r
]]></code-block>
        <p>
            The developers who had the libraries in their global workspace replace the entry with
            <code>folder: ~/work/ws-acme</code>. One line gives them every project of the company, from any folder.
        </p>

        <chapter title="Working in one team or in the whole company" id="use-cases_chapter-company-scope">
            <p>
                From <path>ws-acme/ws-dev1</path>, the <a href="Scope.topic">scope</a> decides how far a command
                goes:
            </p>
            <table>
                <tr>
                    <td>Command</td>
                    <td>Reaches</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><code>%tool_call% search lib</code></td>
                    <td>dev1 only: no match</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><code>%tool_call% search lib -s topmost</code></td>
                    <td>The company: <code>lib-back</code>, <code>lib-front</code></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><code>%tool_call% ff</code></td>
                    <td>The 2 projects of dev1</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><code>%tool_call% ff -s topmost</code></td>
                    <td>The 6 projects of the company, in parallel</td>
                </tr>
            </table>
        </chapter>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="What made it work" id="use-cases_chapter-recap">
        <table>
            <tr>
                <td>Need</td>
                <td>Feature</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>A team gets its projects in two commands</td>
                <td>A <a href="Workspace-file.topic">workspace file</a> in a git repository, and <code>update</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Reuse the work of another team</td>
                <td>A sub-workspace, loaded with <code>update -r</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Reach projects without imposing it on others</td>
                <td>The personal global workspace</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Onboard anybody on the whole company</td>
                <td>A company workspace that lists the team workspaces</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Act on one team or on everything</td>
                <td>The <a href="Scope.topic">scope</a>: <code>nearest</code> or <code>topmost</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Jump to any project</td>
                <td><code>%tool_call% search</code> and <code>gcd</code></td>
            </tr>
        </table>
        <tip>
            Add <a href="Hook.topic">hooks</a> to the workspace repositories, for example to install dependencies
            after each clone. The whole team gets them with the next <code>git pull</code>.
        </tip>
    </chapter>

